PG.notes("l234", "<p class=\"summary\">Opens the section on writing your own API. Covers why APIs are worth building and selling, the three sources of API value, public versus internal APIs, and the five REST constraints, then previews the joke API built over the next lectures.</p>\n<h3>Why build or consume APIs</h3>\n<ul><li>An API lets a developer reuse someone else's data, service or algorithm instead of collecting data or building the service from scratch, so a product can reach a first working version in minutes.</li><li>Public APIs are open to anyone who registers or holds credentials; some are free, some paid. Marketplaces such as RapidAPI host third-party APIs and bill consumers per request, and also list free ones.</li><li>Marketplace-hosted APIs usually keep their source closed so others cannot just clone them. Open-source APIs (the Domino's Pizza Node.js API on GitHub is the example given) can be read to see how a real one is built.</li></ul>\n<h3>What makes an API valuable enough to charge for</h3>\n<ul><li>Data collection: a large or rare dataset (a big recipe archive, live weather-station readings).</li><li>Algorithm or service: logic that is costly to reproduce, e.g. a route-distance calculator combining mapping APIs, or a large trained AI model exposed through an endpoint.</li><li>Simplified interface: wrapping something awkward behind a few parameters, e.g. ordering a pizza with location and payment inputs, or reducing Google Sheets/Maps to the two or three operations most people need so it takes a few lines of code instead of hundreds.</li></ul>\n<h3>Internal (private) APIs</h3>\n<ul><li>Every backend built so far in the course is effectively an internal API: it serves the company's own front end, is undocumented for outsiders and carries no support promise.</li><li>Internal does not mean secure. Without authentication or other protection, anyone who discovers the endpoints can call them, and people do reverse-engineer private APIs. Keep this in mind both when building and when investigating how a site works.</li></ul>\n<h3>REST: the five constraints</h3>\n<ul><li>REST stands for Representational State Transfer. 'What makes an API RESTful?' is a classic interview question.</li><li>1. Standard HTTP methods: GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH partially updates, DELETE removes.</li><li>2. Standard response format: resources are sent back as a representation, most commonly JSON (or XML). This is the 'representation' in the name.</li><li>3. Client/server separation: the two live on different systems, talk only over the network by request and response, and can be built, deployed and scaled independently.</li><li>4. Statelessness: every request carries everything the server needs to answer it. The server stores no per-client memory between requests (for example it must not assume that a client who got item 4 now wants item 5). This keeps the server simple and lets it serve many clients.</li><li>5. Resource-based: data is modelled as resources, each located by a URI/URL. A URL is a kind of URI that gives the address of a resource.</li><li>The web itself fits all five constraints (URLs as resources, HTTP, standard formats, separate browser and server, stateless requests), which is why it is the standard example of REST.</li></ul>\n<h3>What gets built next</h3>\n<ul><li>A RESTful joke API: the server holds a collection of jokes, each tagged with a category, and clients can fetch, add, replace, amend and delete them using the HTTP methods above.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> How the HTTP verbs map to actions on a jokes resource</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">GET    /jokes/5     -&gt; read joke 5\nPOST   /jokes       -&gt; create a new joke\nPUT    /jokes/5     -&gt; replace joke 5 entirely\nPATCH  /jokes/5     -&gt; change some fields of joke 5\nDELETE /jokes/5     -&gt; remove joke 5</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Treating an undocumented internal API as private; without auth it can still be called by anyone who finds it.</li><li>Storing per-client state on the server between requests breaks the stateless constraint.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Mixing up URI and URL: every URL is a URI but not the reverse.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l235", "<p class=\"summary\">Sets up the Postman collection and starter project for the joke API, then builds the three GET endpoints: a random joke, a joke by id (path parameter) and jokes filtered by type (query parameter). Each is a challenge with a walkthrough of the solution.</p>\n<h3>Setup: Postman collection, docs and starter project</h3>\n<ul><li>Download the lesson's postman_collection.json from the course resources, open Postman, go to Workspaces &gt; My Workspace &gt; Collections &gt; Import and drop the file in. It creates a JokeAPI collection with one request for each endpoint planned for the section: GET random, GET by id, GET filtered by type, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE one, DELETE all.</li><li>Postman can publish auto-generated documentation for a collection (the three-dots menu &gt; View documentation). The docs list each request, an example cURL command (Axios and other snippets are also offered) and an example response. Saving requests into a collection is all that is needed to generate them.</li><li>The response shape for every joke is a JSON object with id, jokeText and jokeType.</li><li>Starter files are in the lesson zip (the DIY API folder). index.js contains the Express/body-parser setup, empty route stubs commented as numbered challenges, and a 100-item jokes array at the bottom of the file. solution.js is the finished server.</li><li>To run the finished version: cd into the folder, run npm i to install the dependencies from package.json, then start it with nodemon solution.js. It listens on port 3000 and every collection request can be sent against it to see the expected behaviour. Run your own index.js the same way while working through the challenges.</li><li>The API is public, so the documented endpoint paths and parameter names (for example the query key type) form a contract that clients rely on and that must be spelled exactly.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge 1: GET /random</h3>\n<ul><li>app.get(route, callback) registers a handler for GET requests. First argument is the route string, second is a (req, res) callback.</li><li>Pick a random index: Math.random() gives a decimal from 0 up to (but not including) 1; multiply by jokes.length to scale it to the array size; Math.floor() rounds down to a valid integer index from 0 to length-1.</li><li>Return the chosen element with res.json(...), which serialises the JavaScript object to JSON text and sends it with a JSON content type.</li><li>Each Send in Postman gives a different joke from the array.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge 2: GET /jokes/:id (path parameter)</h3>\n<ul><li>A colon in a route (/jokes/:id) declares a path parameter. Whatever sits in that URL position is available as req.params.id. In Postman it is filled in through the Params tab &gt; Path Variables, producing a URL like localhost:3000/jokes/2.</li><li>req.params holds values from the path itself; req.query holds values after the question mark (?id=2). This route uses params, so req.query is not involved.</li><li>Path parameters always arrive as strings, but the id fields in the jokes array are numbers. Strict equality (===) compares type as well as value, so '2' === 2 is false.</li><li>Two fixes: use loose equality (==), which converts types, or convert the string with parseInt() and keep ===. The instructor prefers the second because nothing is left implicit.</li><li>Array.prototype.find(callback) loops over the array, passes each element to the callback and returns the first element for which the callback returns true (or undefined if none match). Here the condition is joke.id === id.</li><li>Any loop (for, forEach) would work; find is just short.</li><li>Send the found object back with res.json.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge 3: GET /filter?type=... (query parameter)</h3>\n<ul><li>The client supplies the category as a query parameter, so the handler reads req.query.type. In Postman, add it under Params &gt; Query Parameters and it is appended to the URL automatically; several can be added.</li><li>Array.prototype.filter(callback) works like find but returns a new array containing every element that passes the test, so the response is an array of all jokes whose jokeType matches. Joke types in the data include Science, Puns, Wordplay, Math, Food, Sports and Movies.</li><li>Return the array with res.json. If nothing matches the result is an empty array.</li></ul>\n<h3>Working method the instructor stresses</h3>\n<ul><li>Each challenge can be attempted solo (pause, try it) or followed via the walkthrough; either way, write the code yourself afterwards in the matching numbered slot in index.js.</li><li>Copying from docs or Q&amp;A sites is fine in real work, provided you understand every part before using it; look up unfamiliar methods on MDN.</li><li>Finish by running index.js with nodemon and re-sending all three GET requests, checking for 200 responses and the expected data.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Starter setup (reference repo index.js / solution.js)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; {\n  console.log(`Successfully started server on port ${port}.`);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">The three GET routes (from the reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// 1. GET a random joke\napp.get(&quot;/random&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);\n  res.json(jokes[randomIndex]);\n});\n\n// 2. GET a specific joke by id (path parameter)\napp.get(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const foundJoke = jokes.find((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  res.json(foundJoke);\n});\n\n// 3. GET jokes filtered by type (query parameter)\napp.get(&quot;/filter&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const type = req.query.type;\n  const filteredJokes = jokes.filter((joke) =&gt; joke.jokeType === type);\n  res.json(filteredJokes);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Shape of a joke object in the data array</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">{\n  id: 1,\n  jokeText: &quot;Why don't scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything.&quot;,\n  jokeType: &quot;Science&quot;,\n}</pre>\n<h2>The task: DIY API: the three GET endpoints</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Lesson zip with index.js (server boilerplate, numbered challenge comments, a 100-joke array at the bottom), solution.js, package.json, plus the imported JokeAPI Postman collection.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Implement GET /random, GET /jokes/:id and GET /filter?type= so that the matching requests in the Postman collection return the same results as solution.js.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Import postman_collection.json into Postman and read the generated docs for the three GET requests.</li><li>Run npm i in the project folder, then run nodemon solution.js and send each GET request to see the target behaviour; stop it afterwards.</li><li>Open index.js and write app.get('/random', ...) under challenge 1: compute a random index with Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length) and respond with res.json(jokes[index]).</li><li>Under challenge 2 write app.get('/jokes/:id', ...): read req.params.id, convert it with parseInt, find the joke whose id matches, and respond with res.json.</li><li>Under challenge 3 write app.get('/filter', ...): read req.query.type, use filter to keep jokes whose jokeType equals it, and respond with the resulting array.</li><li>Run nodemon index.js and send all three requests from Postman, confirming 200 status codes and correct bodies.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> /random returns a different single joke object on repeated sends; /jokes/2 returns the joke with id 2; /filter?type=Puns returns an array of only Puns jokes.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/random&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);\n  res.json(jokes[randomIndex]);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const foundJoke = jokes.find((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  res.json(foundJoke);\n});\n\napp.get(&quot;/filter&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const type = req.query.type;\n  const filteredJokes = jokes.filter((joke) =&gt; joke.jokeType === type);\n  res.json(filteredJokes);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Comparing the string from req.params.id with a numeric id using ===, which never matches; convert with parseInt first.</li><li>Misspelling the query key (type) so req.query.type is undefined and the filter returns an empty array.</li><li>Confusing req.params (path) with req.query (after the ?).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> /jokes/:id with an unknown id makes find return undefined and the client receives an empty reply with no error status; the lecture does not handle this case.</li><li>Copy-pasting a solution without understanding each method.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l236", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds the three write endpoints of the joke API in the DIY project: POST /jokes to add a joke, PUT /jokes/:id to replace one completely, and PATCH /jokes/:id to change only the supplied fields. Each is a challenge followed by a walkthrough.</p>\n<h3>Challenge 4: POST /jokes (create)</h3>\n<ul><li>The client sends the new joke in the request body as x-www-form-urlencoded data with two fields, text and type (set these in Postman's Body tab).</li><li>The id is not supplied by the client. It is generated as jokes.length + 1, so with 100 jokes the next id is 101, then 102 and so on. (Extra) This breaks if jokes are ever deleted, since lengths and ids drift apart; using the highest existing id plus one is safer.</li><li>The starter file already registers body-parser (bodyParser.urlencoded with extended: true) via app.use, which is the middleware that fills req.body. Express has its own built-in parser, but body-parser is kept for clarity and because it is widely used.</li><li>Build a newJoke object with id, jokeText (from req.body.text) and jokeType (from req.body.type), then jokes.push(newJoke) to add it to the end of the array.</li><li>console.log(jokes.slice(-1)) prints the last array element: a negative index to slice counts from the end, and the result is a one-item array.</li><li>Respond with res.json(newJoke) so the client can confirm what was created. The response comes from the object built out of the request, not from re-reading the array; that is a design choice.</li><li>Possible extensions mentioned: validate text length and restrict type to an allowed list. They are not required.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge 5: PUT /jokes/:id (full replacement)</h3>\n<ul><li>PUT means a complete update: every non-automatic field must be sent, and the stored resource is overwritten with them. The id stays the same because it identifies the item.</li><li>Because the whole object is replaced, a field sent blank or left out ends up empty in the stored joke. That is correct PUT behaviour.</li><li>In Postman the id goes in the path (Params &gt; Path Variables, e.g. 2) and text and type go in the form-encoded body.</li><li>Handler steps: read the id from req.params.id (parseInt for strict comparison, or use ==), build replacementJoke = { id, jokeText: req.body.text, jokeType: req.body.type }, locate the position with findIndex, assign jokes[searchIndex] = replacementJoke, then respond with the new object.</li><li>Array.prototype.findIndex(callback) is like find, but returns the index of the first match (or -1 if none), which is what is needed to overwrite an element in place.</li><li>A real API would normally implement either PUT or PATCH for updates; both are shown here for practice.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge 6: PATCH /jokes/:id (partial update)</h3>\n<ul><li>PATCH changes only part of a resource and is what most updates really need (e.g. change one field of a user without resending everything).</li><li>The example goal: change the type of joke 2 to a new category such as Agriculture while leaving its text alone.</li><li>Handler steps: parseInt the id, find the existing joke, then build replacementJoke with each field set to req.body.field || existingJoke.field. The || operator keeps the value from the request when it is present (truthy) and otherwise falls back to the stored value.</li><li>Then findIndex on the same id, overwrite jokes[searchIndex] with replacementJoke, and send it back as JSON.</li><li>The same logic could be written with if statements for readability; the || form is a common compact idiom. Test it in a console if it is unfamiliar.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The || fallback cannot set a field to an empty string or other falsy value; ?? or an explicit undefined check handles that.</li></ul>\n<h3>Verifying</h3>\n<ul><li>Run index.js with nodemon and send the POST, PUT and PATCH requests from the JokeAPI collection (check both the Params and Body tabs, which are pre-filled). Before the routes exist Postman shows an error; once they work it shows the expected JSON.</li><li>Re-read any unfamiliar method (slice, find, findIndex) on MDN before moving on.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">POST a new joke (reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/jokes&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const newJoke = {\n    id: jokes.length + 1,\n    jokeText: req.body.text,\n    jokeType: req.body.type,\n  };\n  jokes.push(newJoke);\n  console.log(jokes.slice(-1));\n  res.json(newJoke);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">PUT: replace a joke entirely (reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.put(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const replacementJoke = {\n    id: id,\n    jokeText: req.body.text,\n    jokeType: req.body.type,\n  };\n  const searchIndex = jokes.findIndex((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  jokes[searchIndex] = replacementJoke;\n  res.json(replacementJoke);\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">PATCH: update only the fields that were sent (reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.patch(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const existingJoke = jokes.find((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  const replacementJoke = {\n    id: id,\n    jokeText: req.body.text || existingJoke.jokeText,\n    jokeType: req.body.type || existingJoke.jokeType,\n  };\n  const searchIndex = jokes.findIndex((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  jokes[searchIndex] = replacementJoke;\n  res.json(replacementJoke);\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: DIY API: POST, PUT and PATCH routes</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> The DIY API project from the previous lecture with the three GET routes done; index.js has empty slots for challenges 4-6 and body-parser already configured; the JokeAPI Postman collection has the POST, PUT and PATCH requests ready.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Implement POST /jokes, PUT /jokes/:id and PATCH /jokes/:id so the Postman requests succeed and the in-memory jokes array changes accordingly.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Challenge 4: add app.post('/jokes', ...) that builds newJoke with id jokes.length + 1 and text/type from req.body, pushes it onto jokes, logs the last item and responds with the new joke.</li><li>Challenge 5: add app.put('/jokes/:id', ...) that parses the id, builds a replacement object from the id and body fields, uses findIndex to locate the joke, overwrites that slot and responds with the replacement.</li><li>Challenge 6: add app.patch('/jokes/:id', ...) that finds the existing joke and builds a replacement using req.body.x || existing.x for text and type, writes it back by index and responds with it.</li><li>Run nodemon index.js and send the POST, PUT and PATCH requests from the Postman collection, checking Params and Body.</li><li>Re-send a GET for the changed id (or filter by type) to confirm the data actually changed.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> POST returns the new joke with the next id; PUT with id 2 returns joke 2 fully replaced (blank fields become empty); PATCH with only type=Agriculture returns joke 2 with the new type and original text.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/jokes&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const newJoke = {\n    id: jokes.length + 1,\n    jokeText: req.body.text,\n    jokeType: req.body.type,\n  };\n  jokes.push(newJoke);\n  res.json(newJoke);\n});\n\napp.put(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const replacementJoke = {\n    id: id,\n    jokeText: req.body.text,\n    jokeType: req.body.type,\n  };\n  const searchIndex = jokes.findIndex((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  jokes[searchIndex] = replacementJoke;\n  res.json(replacementJoke);\n});\n\napp.patch(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const existingJoke = jokes.find((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  const replacementJoke = {\n    id: id,\n    jokeText: req.body.text || existingJoke.jokeText,\n    jokeType: req.body.type || existingJoke.jokeType,\n  };\n  const searchIndex = jokes.findIndex((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  jokes[searchIndex] = replacementJoke;\n  res.json(replacementJoke);\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Sending the body as JSON or raw in Postman when the server only has the urlencoded parser, leaving req.body fields undefined; use x-www-form-urlencoded.</li><li>Forgetting parseInt on the id, so === never finds a match and findIndex returns -1 (writing jokes[-1] silently adds a bogus property).</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> PATCH on an unknown id: existingJoke is undefined and reading its properties throws; no 404 handling is shown.</li><li>Mistaking PUT for a partial update; omitted fields are wiped.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Data lives only in memory, so restarts (nodemon reloads) reset all additions and edits.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l237", "<p class=\"summary\">Finishes the joke API with two DELETE routes: remove one joke by id, and wipe the whole collection only when the caller supplies the correct master key as a query parameter, a minimal form of API-key protection.</p>\n<h3>Challenge 7: DELETE /jokes/:id</h3>\n<ul><li>Uses Express's app.delete(route, callback). The id arrives as a path variable in req.params.id; convert with parseInt and use ===, or skip the conversion and use ==.</li><li>Locate the joke with findIndex, which returns -1 when nothing matches.</li><li>If the index is greater than -1, jokes.splice(searchIndex, 1) removes exactly one element starting at that index (splice mutates the array in place), then res.sendStatus(200) replies OK.</li><li>Otherwise reply with res.status(404).json({ error: ... }) saying the joke was not found and that nothing was deleted.</li><li>A single-joke delete is low risk, so no authentication is applied.</li><li>The solution file leaves an optional prompt about edge cases when deleting by id; the missing-id case handled by the 404 branch is the one it points at.</li></ul>\n<h3>Challenge 8: DELETE /all with a master key</h3>\n<ul><li>Wiping the whole collection is dangerous, so the route demands a secret key sent as a query parameter named key (e.g. /all?key=&lt;code&gt;). In Postman this is under Params &gt; Query Parameters.</li><li>The starter file defines a masterKey constant near the top, representing the secret kept on the server. Later modules cover generating real keys and proper authentication.</li><li>Handler: read req.query.key into userKey and compare with masterKey. If equal, reassign the array to an empty array (jokes = []) and send status 200. If not, respond with a 404 status and a JSON error saying the caller is not authorised.</li><li>Because the handler reassigns jokes, the array must be declared with var or let rather than const (the reference file uses var).</li><li>This is a very simplified key check; it only illustrates guarding risky operations. Real systems add hashed credentials, tokens, HTTPS and so on, which come later in the course.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> A 401 or 403 status is more conventional than 404 for failed authorisation, and sending secrets in the query string leaves them in logs and browser history.</li></ul>\n<h3>Wrap-up</h3>\n<ul><li>Compare your behaviour with solution.js and confirm both DELETE requests in the Postman collection respond as expected. The next lesson applies all of these routes to a blog API.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Delete one joke (reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.delete(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const searchIndex = jokes.findIndex((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  if (searchIndex &gt; -1) {\n    jokes.splice(searchIndex, 1);\n    res.sendStatus(200);\n  } else {\n    res\n      .status(404)\n      .json({ error: `Joke with id: ${id} not found. No jokes were deleted.` });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Delete all jokes, guarded by a master key (reference solution)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">const masterKey = &quot;&lt;server-side secret&gt;&quot;; // defined near the top of the file\n\napp.delete(&quot;/all&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const userKey = req.query.key;\n  if (userKey === masterKey) {\n    jokes = [];\n    res.sendStatus(200);\n  } else {\n    res\n      .status(404)\n      .json({ error: `You are not authorised to perform this action.` });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: DIY API: DELETE routes</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> DIY API project with GET, POST, PUT and PATCH done; index.js has slots for challenges 7 and 8 and a masterKey constant near the top; Postman collection has DELETE a Joke and DELETE All Jokes requests.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Implement DELETE /jokes/:id to remove one joke and DELETE /all to clear all jokes only when the correct key query parameter is supplied.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Challenge 7: add app.delete('/jokes/:id', ...); parse the id and find its index with findIndex.</li><li>If the index exists, splice one element out at that index and respond with status 200; otherwise respond 404 with a JSON error message.</li><li>Challenge 8: add app.delete('/all', ...); read req.query.key.</li><li>If it equals masterKey, set jokes to an empty array and send status 200; otherwise send an error status with a JSON 'not authorised' message.</li><li>Run nodemon index.js and send both DELETE requests from Postman, trying a valid id, an unknown id, a wrong key and the right key; then GET to see the array changed.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Deleting an existing id returns OK 200 and that joke disappears; an unknown id returns a 404 JSON error; /all with the right key returns 200 and leaves an empty collection; a wrong or missing key returns the error and deletes nothing.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.delete(&quot;/jokes/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const id = parseInt(req.params.id);\n  const searchIndex = jokes.findIndex((joke) =&gt; joke.id === id);\n  if (searchIndex &gt; -1) {\n    jokes.splice(searchIndex, 1);\n    res.sendStatus(200);\n  } else {\n    res.status(404).json({ error: `Joke with id: ${id} not found. No jokes were deleted.` });\n  }\n});\n\napp.delete(&quot;/all&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const userKey = req.query.key;\n  if (userKey === masterKey) {\n    jokes = [];\n    res.sendStatus(200);\n  } else {\n    res.status(404).json({ error: `You are not authorised to perform this action.` });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Calling splice(-1, 1) when findIndex returns -1 would delete the last joke; always check the index is greater than -1 first.</li><li>Declaring the jokes array with const and then trying to reassign it to [] in the delete-all route.</li><li>Leaving the key check out so anyone can wipe the data.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Hard-coding a secret in source; the course does this only for teaching.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l238", "<p class=\"summary\">Final project of the module: write the back end of a blog API in index.js so that a prebuilt front-end server (server.js) can list, create, edit and delete posts through it. The lecture explains the two-server setup, the five route challenges, and then walks through the solution.</p>\n<h3>Project shape: two servers, two ports</h3>\n<ul><li>server.js is the website: it runs on port 3000, renders EJS pages and calls the API with Axios. index.js is the API itself and must listen on port 4000, which is where server.js expects to find it.</li><li>With only server.js running, localhost:3000 shows an 'Error fetching posts' message because nothing answers on port 4000 yet.</li><li>Start each server in its own terminal: cd into the project, run npm i once, then nodemon server.js in one and nodemon index.js (or solution.js) in the other.</li><li>Only one process can own a port. Starting index.js while solution.js still runs crashes with EADDRINUSE (address already in use). Stop the other one with Ctrl+C first. At any moment only two terminals should be active: the website on 3000 and one API on 4000.</li><li>solution.js is a complete working API. Running it instead of index.js is a quick way to see the finished app, or to confirm that the front-end works before debugging your own routes.</li></ul>\n<h3>What the API must support</h3>\n<ul><li>Four HTTP verbs: GET, POST, PATCH and DELETE. PUT is deliberately skipped because PATCH already covers the update case (PATCH changes only the supplied fields, PUT would replace the whole record).</li><li>The front-end shows every post in order (title, creation date, author, content), has a form to create a post, an edit form that pre-fills the current values and sends a partial update, and a delete link per post.</li><li>Data is an in-memory array of post objects with id, title, content, author and date, plus a lastId counter. It resets whenever the server restarts. (Extra) Later modules replace this array with a real database.</li><li>Middleware is already wired: bodyParser.json() and bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }), which is what lets req.body hold the fields sent by the HTML forms.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution walkthrough: GET routes</h3>\n<ul><li>GET /posts: respond with res.json(posts), returning the entire array as JSON.</li><li>GET /posts/:id: read the id path parameter from req.params.id, convert it to a number with parseInt (path params are strings), and look it up with posts.find. If found, return it with res.json; if not, respond with status 404 and a JSON message 'Post not found'.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution walkthrough: POST /posts</h3>\n<ul><li>server.js sends the new-post form data to API_URL + '/posts', so the API must define POST /posts.</li><li>Fields come from req.body.title, req.body.content and req.body.author. These names must equal the name attributes of the inputs in views/modify.ejs, which contains two forms: one for creating (POST) and one for editing (PATCH).</li><li>The date is set server-side with new Date(). The id is made by incrementing the lastId counter by one and storing it back, so ids never get reused after deletions.</li><li>The new object is pushed to the posts array and returned as JSON (status 201 is the semantic 'created' code).</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution walkthrough: PATCH /posts/:id</h3>\n<ul><li>Find the post by id as in the GET route; return 404 'Post not found' if it does not exist.</li><li>For each of title, content and author, overwrite the stored value only if a value arrived in req.body. Fields that are missing or empty are left untouched, which is the whole point of PATCH.</li><li>Respond with the updated post as JSON.</li></ul>\n<h3>Solution walkthrough: DELETE /posts/:id</h3>\n<ul><li>In index.ejs the Delete button is a link to /api/post/delete/&lt;id&gt;, generated inside the forEach loop over posts. server.js handles that route and forwards a DELETE request to the API at /posts/&lt;id&gt;, passing the id as a path parameter.</li><li>The API uses findIndex to locate the post. If the index is -1, return 404; otherwise posts.splice(index, 1) removes exactly that one element, then send a confirmation message as JSON.</li></ul>\n<h3>Learning advice from the lecture</h3>\n<ul><li>Nothing in the project is new: EJS views, an Express back end, Axios calls and self-built API routes have all appeared before. The difficulty is joining them mentally.</li><li>Reading someone else's code takes longer than writing your own. If it does not click the first time, redo the project while studying the solution and the other files.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Core of the blog API (index.js), condensed from the reference solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 4000;\n\nlet posts = [ /* { id, title, content, author, date } ... */ ];\nlet lastId = 3;\n\napp.use(bodyParser.json());\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\n\n// 1. GET all posts\napp.get(&quot;/posts&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.json(posts));\n\n// 2. GET one post\napp.get(&quot;/posts/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const post = posts.find((p) =&gt; p.id === parseInt(req.params.id));\n  if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ message: &quot;Post not found&quot; });\n  res.json(post);\n});\n\n// 3. POST a new post\napp.post(&quot;/posts&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  lastId += 1;\n  const post = {\n    id: lastId,\n    title: req.body.title,\n    content: req.body.content,\n    author: req.body.author,\n    date: new Date(),\n  };\n  posts.push(post);\n  res.status(201).json(post);\n});\n\n// 4. PATCH: update only the supplied fields\napp.patch(&quot;/posts/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const post = posts.find((p) =&gt; p.id === parseInt(req.params.id));\n  if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ message: &quot;Post not found&quot; });\n  if (req.body.title) post.title = req.body.title;\n  if (req.body.content) post.content = req.body.content;\n  if (req.body.author) post.author = req.body.author;\n  res.json(post);\n});\n\n// 5. DELETE by id\napp.delete(&quot;/posts/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const index = posts.findIndex((p) =&gt; p.id === parseInt(req.params.id));\n  if (index === -1) return res.status(404).json({ message: &quot;Post not found&quot; });\n  posts.splice(index, 1);\n  res.json({ message: &quot;Post deleted&quot; });\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; console.log(`API is running at http://localhost:${port}`));</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Running the two servers</p><pre data-lang=\"sh\"># terminal 1: the website (port 3000)\ncd &quot;30.03 Blog API Project&quot;\nnpm i\nnodemon server.js\n\n# terminal 2: the API (port 4000)\ncd &quot;30.03 Blog API Project&quot;\nnodemon index.js      # or: nodemon solution.js</pre>\n<h2>The task: Blog API Project</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Download the project, extract it, open it in VS Code and run npm i. server.js (the website on port 3000), the EJS views and the posts array in index.js are provided. index.js has five commented CHALLENGE placeholders and no routes.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Implement the API routes in index.js so the front-end on localhost:3000 can display, create, edit and delete blog posts through the API on localhost:4000.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run npm i, then start server.js with nodemon and confirm localhost:3000 shows an error because no API answers on port 4000.</li><li>In a second terminal start nodemon index.js. Keep solution.js stopped, otherwise EADDRINUSE.</li><li>Challenge 1: GET /posts returns all posts as JSON.</li><li>Challenge 2: GET /posts/:id returns the post with that id, or 404 with a message when absent.</li><li>Challenge 3: POST /posts builds a new post from req.body.title, content and author, adds a new id and the current date, pushes it to the array and returns it.</li><li>Challenge 4: PATCH /posts/:id updates only the fields provided and returns the updated post (404 if not found).</li><li>Challenge 5: DELETE /posts/:id removes the matching post from the array.</li><li>Test in the browser: all posts show, a new post appears at the bottom, an edit changes the content, delete removes the post.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> With nodemon index.js on port 4000 and server.js on port 3000, the blog UI lists posts and create, edit and delete all work.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/posts&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.json(posts));\n\napp.get(&quot;/posts/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const post = posts.find((p) =&gt; p.id === parseInt(req.params.id));\n  if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ message: &quot;Post not found&quot; });\n  res.json(post);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/posts&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  lastId += 1;\n  const post = { id: lastId, title: req.body.title, content: req.body.content, author: req.body.author, date: new Date() };\n  posts.push(post);\n  res.status(201).json(post);\n});\n\napp.patch(&quot;/posts/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const post = posts.find((p) =&gt; p.id === parseInt(req.params.id));\n  if (!post) return res.status(404).json({ message: &quot;Post not found&quot; });\n  if (req.body.title) post.title = req.body.title;\n  if (req.body.content) post.content = req.body.content;\n  if (req.body.author) post.author = req.body.author;\n  res.json(post);\n});\n\napp.delete(&quot;/posts/:id&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const index = posts.findIndex((p) =&gt; p.id === parseInt(req.params.id));\n  if (index === -1) return res.status(404).json({ message: &quot;Post not found&quot; });\n  posts.splice(index, 1);\n  res.json({ message: &quot;Post deleted&quot; });\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Running two servers on port 4000 gives EADDRINUSE; stop solution.js before starting index.js.</li><li>Forgetting parseInt: req.params.id is a string, so a strict === comparison with a numeric id never matches.</li><li>Body field names in req.body must match the name attributes in modify.ejs exactly.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference index.js builds the id as posts.length + 1, which can duplicate an existing id after a deletion. Use the lastId counter as solution.js does.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The in-memory array is wiped at every nodemon restart, so edits made during testing disappear.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l239", "<p class=\"summary\">Short career tip for learners aiming at a developer job: demonstrated skills and collaboration matter more than formal credentials.</p>\n<h3>Key takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Most employers weigh what a candidate can do above where they studied. A minority have hard degree filters, but they are the exception.</li><li>Build a portfolio. Browse job listings on sites like Indeed or LinkedIn, note the skills requested, and create projects that show those skills.</li><li>Show teamwork as well: answer questions on Stack Overflow or in the course Q&amp;A, and take part in code reviews with more senior developers.</li><li>Good team members work with people both above them (to learn from) and below them (to teach). Building a visible record of both helps convince recruiters.</li></ul>");
